title: MOVEit / Cl0p
page: breaches/moveit-cl0p.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Cl0p exploited an unauthenticated SQL injection zero-day in MOVEit Transfer; reporting places the eventual toll at more than 2,700 organizations and 95 million people.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 2,770 | organizations affected, approximately | per Emsisoft's tally, June 2024
stat: 95.8M | individuals affected, approximately | in the same Emsisoft tally
stat: Days | not months: how long the campaign took | per Mandiant's assessment
stat: None | authentication required | no credential, no phishing, no insider
Cl0p exploited CVE-2023-34362 in Progress Software's MOVEit Transfer as a zero-day. The page calls MOVEit the third time: Accellion, GoAnywhere, then MOVEit, the same playbook.
notes:
Start with the page's headline figures, and with who stands behind each one.

The first two come from Emsisoft's tally as of June 2024: about 2,770 organizations and about 95.8 million individuals affected. The page's summary puts it more loosely: reporting places the eventual toll at more than 2,700 organizations and 95 million people. The page's headline tile says these came from one flaw.

The third figure is a length of time. In Mandiant's framing, the campaign was measured in days rather than months.

The fourth is about the way in. CVE-2023-34362 needed no authentication: no credential, no phishing and no insider. A CVE is a unique ID for a publicly disclosed vulnerability. A zero-day is a flaw attackers exploit before the vendor has released a fix, and the page says Cl0p exploited this one as a zero-day.

The page calls MOVEit the third time Cl0p ran the same playbook, after Accellion and GoAnywhere. That pattern comes back after the stages.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: May 9, 2023 | Fastly observes the earliest injection attempts
event: May 27 | Mandiant sees exploitation from this date
event: May 31 | Progress warns customers
event: June 2 | The CVE is assigned
event: June 5 | Microsoft attributes the campaign to Lace Tempest, a Cl0p affiliate
event: June 2024 | Emsisoft's tally: ~2,770 organizations, ~95.8M individuals
Disclosures ran for well over a year as downstream victims were identified. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those.

Fastly observed the earliest injection attempts on May 9, 2023. Mandiant saw exploitation from May 27. Progress Software, which makes MOVEit Transfer, warned customers on May 31, and the CVE was assigned on June 2. On June 5, Microsoft attributed the campaign to Lace Tempest, a Cl0p affiliate.

Keep the page's words for the first two markers. May 9 is when Fastly observed injection attempts, and May 27 is when Mandiant saw exploitation.

The last marker is a count rather than an event in the campaign: Emsisoft's tally as of June 2024. The page says the disclosure tail ran for well over a year, as downstream victims discovered that their data had been held by someone else's MOVEit server.

The page gives no end date for the campaign itself. The markers are evenly spaced, so read them as order only.

== Which figure counts what
layout: table
row: Figure | From | What it counts
row: ~2,674 | Palo Alto's Xpanse | MOVEit servers exposing HTTP or HTTPS at the time
row: Over a thousand | The kill chain, stage 04 | Organizations compromised in a campaign measured in days
row: ~2,770 | Emsisoft's tally, June 2024 | Organizations affected, including downstream victims
row: ~95.8M | Emsisoft's tally, June 2024 | Individuals affected
Each row counts something different. A count of exposed servers is not a count of victims.
notes:
Slow down here. The page gives several figures in different places, and each one counts something different.

About 2,674 is the exposed population: MOVEit servers exposing HTTP or HTTPS at the time, as Palo Alto's Xpanse counted them. It counts servers that could be reached, not organizations that were compromised.

Over a thousand is the page's own figure, in stage 04, for organizations Cl0p compromised in a campaign measured in days.

About 2,770 organizations and about 95.8 million individuals is Emsisoft's tally as of June 2024, which the page uses for the scale of the impact. It includes downstream victims: organizations whose data sat on a MOVEit server run by someone else, such as a payroll or benefits provider.

When you repeat a figure, say what it counts and who counted it.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Targeting | Software that aggregates others' data | T1594
step: 02 | Initial Access | Unauthenticated SQL injection | T1190
step: 03 | Persistence | The LEMURLOOT web shell | T1505.003
step: 04 | Exfiltration | Days, not months | T1213
step: 05 | Impact | Extortion without encryption | T1657
Arrows follow the page's stage order.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's five stages, each with its phase and the first ATT&CK technique the page lists.

Stage 01 is targeting: why managed file transfer software. Stage 02 is the way in, a SQL injection flaw that needed no authentication. Stage 03 is persistence, the LEMURLOOT web shell. Stage 04, under the page's Exfiltration phase, is the tempo of a campaign measured in days. Stage 05 is the impact: extortion with nothing encrypted, and a disclosure tail of well over a year.

The arrows follow the page's stage order. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Why Cl0p targets managed file transfer software
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Targeting
technique: T1594 | Search Victim-Owned Websites
fact: Why MFT | internet-facing by design; regulated data, in one database
fact: Prior targets | Accellion FTA (2021), GoAnywhere MFT (2023)
fact: Indirect victims | organizations whose payroll provider or insurer ran it
fact: Aggravating factor | retention rarely configured, so old files were still present
One unauthenticated MFT flaw yields regulated data from hundreds of organizations.
notes:
Stage 01 is target selection. The page maps it to T1594, Search Victim-Owned Websites.

MFT stands for managed file transfer, and FTA for File Transfer Appliance. The page says Cl0p's choice of Accellion FTA, GoAnywhere MFT and now MOVEit is a strategy rather than coincidence. Managed file transfer products must be reachable from the internet so partners can upload to them. They are deployed at organizations that handle regulated data, because that is why you buy one instead of using email. And they hold that data in bulk, concentrated in one database, often for far longer than anyone intended, because retention was never configured. Regulated data is data that laws or regulations govern, and retention is how long files are kept before they are deleted.

So, the page says, one unauthenticated flaw in such a product yields regulated data from hundreds of organizations, without any of the per-victim effort a normal campaign requires. The victims include entities that never bought MOVEit at all, because their payroll provider or insurer did.

== CVE-2023-34362: unauthenticated SQL injection that reached the underlying database
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1190 | Exploit Public-Facing Application
fact: Flaw | SQL injection in MOVEit Transfer's web application
fact: Authentication | none required
fact: Reached | the underlying database, not just application functions
fact: Preventable by | parameterized queries, application security's oldest advice
With no authentication, controls that depend on knowing who is asking fall away.
notes:
Stage 02 is the way in. The page maps it to T1190, Exploit Public-Facing Application.

CVE-2023-34362 let an unauthenticated attacker inject SQL into MOVEit Transfer's web application and reach the underlying database directly. Cl0p exploited it as a zero-day.

The page asks you to pause on this: SQL injection, in 2023, in a product built to custody sensitive files. It says the class has been at the top of the OWASP list for most of two decades and is entirely preventable with parameterized queries. OWASP is the Open Worldwide Application Security Project.

The page adds two details. The flaw was reachable without authentication, which removes every compensating control that depends on knowing who is asking. And it led to the database rather than merely to the application.

Related flaws, CVE-2023-35036 and CVE-2023-35708, were found and patched over the following weeks. The page calls that the usual pattern once researchers start looking hard at a codebase that has just been shown to have one.

The next slide explains SQL injection and parameterized queries in general terms.

== What SQL injection is, and what parameterized queries do
layout: background
source: OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet | https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html
OWASP says SQL injection happens when untrusted data becomes part of a query's SQL syntax and changes its logic.

Its main defense is the parameterized query: define the SQL structure separately, then bind the data values through the database API.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general idea from OWASP's cheat sheet and says nothing about the incident.

SQL is the language applications use to query a database. OWASP says a SQL injection attack occurs when untrusted data, usually strings, becomes part of a query's SQL syntax, modifying the logic of the request. The data can come from an HTTP request, from another service, or even from the database itself. Depending on the database's features and privileges, a successful attack can read data from the database or modify it.

OWASP's resolution is to define the SQL structure separately from its data values, then bind those values through the database API, which is what a parameterized query does. A value bound that way is treated as a literal value, so it cannot change the query's syntax. OWASP strongly discourages escaping input by hand instead, because escaping rules differ between databases and the approach is error-prone.

Nothing here describes this flaw beyond the page. The page says CVE-2023-34362 was a SQL injection flaw in MOVEit Transfer's web application, reachable without authentication, that led to the underlying database.

== LEMURLOOT blended in, and patching alone did not remove it
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Persistence
technique: T1505.003 | Server Software Component: Web Shell
technique: T1036.005 | Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location
fact: Implant | a web shell named to resemble legitimate MOVEit components
fact: Location | the application's own directory structure
fact: Capability | database enumeration, file retrieval, stolen-data management
fact: Required action | hunt for the web shell, independently of applying the fix
Patching closes the door but does not evict whoever already walked through it.
notes:
Stage 03 is persistence. The page maps it to T1505.003, Server Software Component: Web Shell, and to T1036.005, Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location, a form of masquerading.

Attackers installed a web shell tracked as LEMURLOOT. It was written into the application's own directory structure, with a name chosen to blend in with genuine MOVEit files, which is why casual inspection of the server did not reveal it. It gave the operators the ability to enumerate the database, retrieve file listings and contents, and manage stolen data. The page calls it effectively a purpose-built administrative console for theft, sitting inside the application.

It survived the initial patch for anyone who applied the fix without hunting for existing compromise. The page calls that the recurring lesson of every mass-exploitation event: patching closes the door but does not evict whoever already walked through it.

The next slide explains web shells and masquerading in general terms.

== What a web shell is, and what masquerading means
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1505.003 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/003/
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1036.005 (attack.mitre.org)
ATT&CK says adversaries may backdoor web servers with web shells, web scripts placed on an openly accessible server, to keep persistent access.

One form of masquerading matches or approximates a legitimate file's name or location, to evade defenses and observation.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK says adversaries may backdoor web servers with web shells to establish persistent access to systems. A web shell is a web script placed on an openly accessible web server, and it may provide a set of functions to execute on the system that hosts the web server.

ATT&CK's T1036.005, Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location, is a form of masquerading. Adversaries may match or approximate the name or location of legitimate files or other resources when naming or placing them, for the sake of evading defenses and observation. Its examples include placing an executable in a commonly trusted directory, or giving it the name of a legitimate, trusted program.

Nothing here describes the incident beyond the page. The page says LEMURLOOT sat within the application's own directory structure, named to resemble legitimate MOVEit components. It does not say how the shell was installed.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1036.005: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1036/005/

== Cl0p compromised over a thousand organizations in a campaign measured in days
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
fact: Exposed population | ~2,674 MOVEit servers at the time, per Palo Alto's Xpanse
fact: Duration | days, per Mandiant's assessment
fact: Sequence | harvest quietly first, extort publicly later
fact: Defender problem | the harvest outran the patch cycle across the whole population
Against scripted, internet-wide exploitation, only controls already in place help.
notes:
Stage 04 is the tempo. The page maps it to T1213, Data from Information Repositories, under its Exfiltration phase.

The page calls the tempo the operational lesson. Because exploitation was automated and required no per-target work, the actors moved through the exposed population faster than defenders could patch. Palo Alto's Xpanse counted around 2,674 MOVEit servers exposing HTTP or HTTPS at the time. That is a count of exposed servers, not of victims. The harvest, the page says, was largely complete before most organizations had finished reading the advisory.

The page calls this the same argument the site makes in its pattern on machine-speed chains, arriving two years before the AI-assisted cases. When exploitation is scripted against an internet-wide population, patch promptly is competing against a process that does not sleep, and the only controls that help are the ones already in place.

The sequence was harvest quietly first, extort publicly later. The extortion is the next stage.

== Extortion without encryption, and a disclosure tail of well over a year
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Scale | ~2,770 organizations, ~95.8M people (Emsisoft, June 2024)
fact: Method | data theft and leak-site extortion; no encryption
fact: Disclosure tail | well over a year, as downstream victims were identified
fact: Typical victim | an organization whose payroll or benefits provider ran MOVEit
The unanswerable question: which of my suppliers hold my data, on what software?
notes:
Stage 05 is the impact. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft.

Nothing was encrypted. Cl0p named victims on a leak site and demanded payment to avoid publication, which the page says is the model the group has run since Accellion. A leak site is a website where an extortion group names its victims.

The disclosure tail ran for well over a year, and the page calls its shape the part worth internalizing. Many affected organizations had never heard of MOVEit, because the server belonged to their payroll processor, their benefits administrator, their pension provider or a state agency. Victims included government departments, universities, banks and health systems, most of them at second or third hand.

The page's conclusion: if you cannot enumerate which of your suppliers hold your data and what software they run it on, you cannot answer whether an event like this affects you. In 2023, it says, almost nobody could.

The scale on the slide is Emsisoft's tally as of June 2024: about 2,770 organizations and about 95.8 million individuals.

The next slide explains, in general terms, why ATT&CK files extortion under Financial Theft.

== Why ATT&CK files extortion under Financial Theft
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1657 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1657/
ATT&CK says adversaries may steal money from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods. Its ransomware example encrypts and steals data, then threatens a leak unless paid. The page says nothing was encrypted here.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

Financial Theft sits under the Impact tactic. ATT&CK says adversaries may steal monetary resources from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft, or other methods aimed at their own financial gain. It calls financial theft the ultimate objective of several popular campaign types, including extortion by ransomware, business email compromise and fraud.

ATT&CK's extortion example is ransomware: an adversary demands payment after encrypting data and exfiltrating it, then threatens to leak sensitive data to the public unless payment is made.

Keep the difference in mind. ATT&CK's example includes encryption. The page says nothing was encrypted in this campaign: Cl0p named victims on a leak site and demanded payment to avoid publication.

== The template, as the page draws it
layout: layers
layer: Find | One unauthenticated flaw in software that aggregates other people's data
layer: Harvest | Quietly, at scale
layer: Extort | Everyone at once
The page says Oracle EBS repeated this template two years later, and that Accellion and GoAnywhere came before MOVEit.
notes:
The page draws one pattern from this chain, and calls it a template.

Find one unauthenticated flaw in software that aggregates other people's data. Harvest quietly at scale. Then extort everyone at once. The arrows follow that order, as the page gives it.

The page says this is the template that Oracle EBS repeated two years later, and that the ones before it were Accellion and GoAnywhere. It calls MOVEit the third time: Accellion, GoAnywhere, then MOVEit, the same playbook.

The next slide puts that series in order.

== Accellion, GoAnywhere, MOVEit, then Oracle EBS
layout: timeline
event: 2021 | Accellion FTA, a prior Cl0p target
event: 2023 | GoAnywhere MFT, a prior Cl0p target
event: May-June 2023 | MOVEit Transfer: the third time, the page says
event: Two years later | Oracle EBS repeats the template, the page says
Accellion, GoAnywhere, then MOVEit: the same playbook, the page says. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page places MOVEit in a series.

Cl0p's prior targets, the page says, were Accellion FTA in 2021 and GoAnywhere MFT in 2023. MOVEit, in May and June 2023, was the third time, with the same playbook. Two years later, the page says, Oracle EBS repeated the template. EBS is Oracle E-Business Suite.

The page gives years, not dates, for the first two, and the markers are evenly spaced, so read them as order only.

== Six defenses against five stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05
row: MFT off the open internet, or behind your own authentication | 01 | 02
row: Short retention: delete the files | 01
row: Hunt for the implant, apart from patching | 03
row: Egress filtering, so a web shell cannot ship data out | 03 | 04
row: Know which suppliers hold your data, on what platform | 05
row: Rehearse the vendor-breach, leak-site scenario | 05
Every stage has at least one defense on the page tied to it.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its five stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something from that stage.

Taking MFT off the open internet cites a product that must be reachable to function, stage 01's reason for the targeting, and authentication you control, where stage 02's flaw needed none. Deleting the files cites the historical data in those databases, stage 01's aggravating factor. Hunting for the implant names LEMURLOOT, stage 03. Egress filtering names a web shell, stage 03, and shipping data out, stage 04's Exfiltration phase. Knowing your suppliers cites downstream victims, most of them at second or third hand in stage 05. Rehearsing the leak-site scenario cites stage 05's extortion.

Two cells stay empty on purpose. The hunting defense also says to close the vulnerability, but its point is the hunt, separate from the patch, so its row leaves stage 02 unmarked. Stage 01 also names indirect victims, but the supplier defense's words, most victims and a year to answer, point at stage 05.

Every stage has at least one tied defense, so the discussion questions come from the defenses themselves.

== Take MFT off the open internet, and delete the files
layout: compare
col: Off the internet (stages 01, 02) | Front MFT with a VPN or identity-aware proxy for internal users | Restrict partner access to known source ranges where possible | Treat remaining public exposure as a documented, monitored exception
col: Delete the files (stage 01) | Damage was proportional to the historical data stored | Configure aggressive automatic deletion: days, not years | Audit what is stored, not what policy says should be
The page calls taking MFT off the internet the only control that worked before a patch existed.
notes:
Two defenses come from stages 01 and 02.

First, take managed file transfer off the open internet, or put it behind authentication you control. Every Cl0p campaign, the page says, has targeted a product that must be reachable to function. Front MFT with a VPN or an identity-aware proxy for internal users, restrict partner access to known source ranges where possible, and treat any remaining public exposure as a documented exception with compensating monitoring. A VPN is a virtual private network. An identity-aware proxy is a gateway that checks who is asking before a request reaches an internal application. The page calls this the only control that worked before a patch existed.

Second, delete the files. The page says retention is a security control here. The damage was proportional to how much historical data sat in those databases, and in most cases far more was present than anyone intended. Configure aggressive automatic deletion on transfer systems, so files live days rather than years, and audit what is stored rather than what policy says should be.

== Hunt for the implant, and filter egress
layout: compare
col: Hunt for the implant (stage 03) | LEMURLOOT persisted through patching for anyone who did not look | Treat an advisory as two tasks: close the vulnerability, and hunt | Assume compromise back to the earliest known exploitation date | Hunt for the published indicators; CISA's advisory carried them
col: Filter egress (stages 03, 04) | A file transfer server has an extremely predictable network profile | Default-deny outbound, with a narrow allow-list | Alert on sustained transfers to unfamiliar destinations
When a mass-exploitation advisory lands, the page treats it as two tasks.
notes:
Two defenses come from stages 03 and 04.

First, hunt for the implant separately from applying the patch. LEMURLOOT persisted through patching for anyone who did not look. When a mass-exploitation advisory lands, the page says, treat it as two tasks: close the vulnerability, and assume compromise back to the earliest known exploitation date, then hunt specifically for the published indicators. Indicators of compromise are artifacts, such as file hashes, tied to known-bad activity. CISA's advisory carried them. CISA is the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency.

Second, filter egress from application servers, so a web shell cannot ship data out. Egress is outbound traffic. The page says a file transfer server has an extremely predictable network profile. Default-deny outbound with a narrow allow-list, and alert on sustained transfers to unfamiliar destinations.

== Know your suppliers, and rehearse the leak site
layout: compare
col: Know your suppliers (stage 05) | Most victims were downstream | Map each vendor to the categories of your data they hold | Ask each vendor which file transfer platform it uses
col: Rehearse the scenario (stage 05) | Your vendor is breached; your data is on a leak site | Decide who validates the claim, and who notifies | Decide when regulatory clocks start, and your position on payment
The extortion is public, fast, and aimed at your customers as much as you, the page says.
notes:
The last two defenses come from stage 05.

First, know which suppliers hold your data, and on what platform. Most victims were downstream. Maintain an inventory mapping each vendor to the categories of your data they hold, and include the question what file transfer platform do you use in vendor assessments. The page calls that the specific question that would have made this answerable in a day rather than a year.

Second, rehearse the scenario in which your vendor was breached and your data is on a leak site. The extortion is public, fast, and aimed at your customers as much as you. Decide in advance who validates the claim, who notifies, when regulatory clocks start, and what your position on payment is. Regulatory clocks are the deadlines for notifying regulators after a breach. The page says the Oracle EBS chain would demand the same preparation of a different set of victims two years later.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Is your file transfer software reachable from the open internet, and why?
check: Does it sit behind a VPN or identity-aware proxy you control?
check: How long do files live on your transfer systems, and what is stored there?
check: After an emergency patch, do you also hunt for an implant?
check: Can your application servers send data anywhere, or only to an allow-list?
check: Could you list which suppliers hold your data, and on what platform?
check: Have you rehearsed your vendor being breached, with your data on a leak site?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether your managed file transfer is reachable from the open internet, why, and whether it sits behind a VPN or an identity-aware proxy you control. Ask how long files live on your transfer systems, and whether anyone has audited what is stored there rather than what policy says.

Ask whether your patching includes a hunt for implants, back to the earliest known exploitation date. Ask whether application servers can send data to any destination or only to a narrow allow-list, and whether sustained transfers to unfamiliar destinations would raise an alert.

Ask whether you could list which suppliers hold your data, and which file transfer platform each one uses. Finally, ask whether you have rehearsed the day a vendor is breached and your data appears on a leak site.

== Your file transfer server is patched. Is it clean?
layout: question
LEMURLOOT survived the initial patch for anyone who applied the fix without hunting for existing compromise.

What would your team do after patching, and how far back would it look?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page says LEMURLOOT was written into the application's own directory structure with a name chosen to blend in with genuine MOVEit files, which is why casual inspection did not reveal it. It survived the initial patch for anyone who applied the fix without hunting for existing compromise.

Ask each person what their team does after an emergency patch, and how far back it looks. Then show the answer slide, which gives what the page says to do.

== What the page says to do after the patch
layout: answer
Treat a mass-exploitation advisory as two tasks.
- Close the vulnerability.
- Assume compromise back to the earliest known exploitation date.
- Hunt for the published indicators; CISA's advisory carried them.
notes:
The page's answer is to treat a mass-exploitation advisory as two tasks: close the vulnerability, and assume compromise back to the earliest known exploitation date, then hunt specifically for the published indicators. CISA's advisory carried them.

For this campaign, the page's summary gives two early dates: Fastly observed the earliest injection attempts on May 9, 2023, and Mandiant saw exploitation from May 27. The page does not say which one counts as the earliest known exploitation date, so if the group asks, say so.

Bring the group's answers back to the page's point: patching closes the door but does not evict whoever already walked through it.

== Would you know if a supplier's MOVEit server held your data?
layout: question
Many affected organizations had never heard of MOVEit: the server belonged to their payroll processor, benefits administrator, pension provider or a state agency.

How quickly could you find out?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page says the disclosure tail ran for well over a year, as downstream victims discovered that their data had been held by someone else's MOVEit server. Victims included government departments, universities, banks and health systems, most of them at second or third hand.

Ask each person how they would find out whether a supplier ran MOVEit with their data on it, and how long that would take. Then show the answer slide.

== What the page says to keep ready
layout: answer
Keep an inventory mapping each vendor to the categories of your data they hold.
- In vendor assessments, ask: what file transfer platform do you use?
- The page says it would have made this answerable in a day, not a year.
notes:
The page's answer is an inventory: map each vendor to the categories of your data they hold, and include the question what file transfer platform do you use in vendor assessments. The page calls that the specific question that would have made this answerable in a day rather than a year.

It also says that in 2023 almost nobody could answer whether an event like this affected them.

Pair it with the rehearsal the page recommends for the day a vendor is breached and your data is on a leak site: decide who validates the claim, who notifies, when regulatory clocks start, and your position on payment.

